   "platform": "x",
   "title": "Federal universities in Nigeria are set to have greater freedom to recruit staff following the Federal Government’s decision to remove the r",
   "url": "https://x.com/thenationpost_/status/2111760496487481496",
   "snippet": "Federal universities in Nigeria are set to have greater freedom to recruit staff following the Federal Government’s decision to remove the requirement for recruitment waivers, President Bola Tinubu has said.\n\nTinubu disclosed the policy change on Saturday through Sunny Echono, Executive Secretary of the Tertiary Education Trust Fund (TETFund), who represented him at the maiden convocation ceremony of the Federal University of Health Sciences, Azare, in Bauchi State.\n\nAccording to the President, the reform is intended to strengthen institutional autonomy and enable universities to recruit academic and non-academic staff according to their manpower needs without seeking approval from the Office of the Head of the Civil Service of the Federation.\n\nHe also said the government was working towards a payroll system better suited to the needs of universities, with the aim of helping them attract and retain qualified personnel.\n\nTinubu’s address also highlighted education funding and research. He disclosed that 1,798 FUHSA students benefited from the Nigerian Education Loan Fund during the 2025/2026 academic session, representing 43 per cent of the university’s student population.\n\nHe further announced that the Federal Government had provided ₦10 billion for the National Research Fund in 2026 through TETFund, urging universities, particularly those specialising in health sciences, to pursue research that addresses Nigeria’s health challenges.\n\nThe President also pledged continued attention to staff welfare, professional development and campus security, including through the Safe School Initiative.\n\nAt the ceremony, FUHSA Vice-Chancellor, Professor Bala Mohammed Audu, said the university had established 22 academic programmes in fewer than five years. Of the 13 programmes that had reached the accreditation stage, 11 received full accreditation while two received interim accreditation.\n\nAudu also announced that the university had graduated 50 pioneer students from its Human Nutrition and Dietetics department, including three first-class graduates. He said the students developed more than 34 value-added nutritional products during their training, some with commercial potential.\n\nThe vice-chancellor called for a review of the decision to extend the nutrition programme from four years to five under the Core Curriculum Minimum Academic Standards.",
   "body": "Federal universities in Nigeria are set to have greater freedom to recruit staff following the Federal Government’s decision to remove the requirement for recruitment waivers, President Bola Tinubu has said.\n\nTinubu disclosed the policy change on Saturday through Sunny Echono, Executive Secretary of the Tertiary Education Trust Fund (TETFund), who represented him at the maiden convocation ceremony of the Federal University of Health Sciences, Azare, in Bauchi State.\n\nAccording to the President, the reform is intended to strengthen institutional autonomy and enable universities to recruit academic and non-academic staff according to their manpower needs without seeking approval from the Office of the Head of the Civil Service of the Federation.\n\nHe also said the government was working towards a payroll system better suited to the needs of universities, with the aim of helping them attract and retain qualified personnel.\n\nTinubu’s address also highlighted education funding and research. He disclosed that 1,798 FUHSA students benefited from the Nigerian Education Loan Fund during the 2025/2026 academic session, representing 43 per cent of the university’s student population.\n\nHe further announced that the Federal Government had provided ₦10 billion for the National Research Fund in 2026 through TETFund, urging universities, particularly those specialising in health sciences, to pursue research that addresses Nigeria’s health challenges.\n\nThe President also pledged continued attention to staff welfare, professional development and campus security, including through the Safe School Initiative.\n\nAt the ceremony, FUHSA Vice-Chancellor, Professor Bala Mohammed Audu, said the university had established 22 academic programmes in fewer than five years. Of the 13 programmes that had reached the accreditation stage, 11 received full accreditation while two received interim accreditation.\n\nAudu also announced that the university had graduated 50 pioneer students from its Human Nutrition and Dietetics department, including three first-class graduates. He said the students developed more than 34 value-added nutritional products during their training, some with commercial potential.\n\nThe vice-chancellor called for a review of the decision to extend the nutrition programme from four years to five under the Core Curriculum Minimum Academic Standards.",
